---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(autodeskSso)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Autodesk acquires BuildingConnected"
    layout="stacked"
    image={platformDiagram}
    imageAlt="Autodesk Construction Cloud as a platform of point solutions: design tools, quantification, collaboration, project management, and BuildingConnected"
    imageCaption="BuildingConnected among Autodesk Construction Cloud’s point solutions."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk acquired BuildingConnected in 2019. BuildingConnected focused on
      bid solicitation, serving both general contractors and subcontractors. By
      targeting the sending and receiving of requests for proposals, it grew
      organically into a large network of construction companies, with a wealth
      of data on trades and pricing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two businesses ran on different models. BuildingConnected was a hybrid
      freemium product and a two-sided market: inviting off-network
      subcontractors led to organic growth and more value, and subcontractors
      and general contractors each owned their own data. Autodesk Construction
      Cloud primarily targeted larger enterprises, where general contractors
      provisioned accounts for their subcontractors and consultants and owned
      the data. Still, BuildingConnected fit well into a platform of point
      solutions, and it stood to benefit from Autodesk’s advanced tools and
      capabilities.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="“One Autodesk”"
    image={accPlatform}
    imageAlt="Autodesk Construction Cloud connecting Autodesk Build, BIM Collaborate, and Docs around shared construction workflows"
    imageCaption="Autodesk Construction Cloud, connecting Build, BIM Collaborate, and Docs."
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk was also trying to create more cohesive product lines and
      internal culture, and to build a unified construction platform. There had
      been talk of keeping BuildingConnected as a separate brand, but that
      clashed with the internal messaging, and it was a source of tension.
      Eventually, it was decided that BuildingConnected should become fully
      embedded in the emerging Autodesk Construction Cloud platform.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="My role">
    <p>
      I was a Senior User Experience Designer at Autodesk from 2019 to 2021,
      working on BuildingConnected. I planned and ran the research for this
      project with a cross-functional tiger team of design, product management,
      and UX research, and led the workshops that aligned the team on direction,
      research synthesis, priorities, and planning.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also designed the critical sign-up and sign-in flows, and developed a
      phased plan for rolling out Autodesk ID that took both the technical
      challenges and users’ incentives and behavior into account.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Why Autodesk ID"
    image={autodeskSignIn}
    imageAlt="The Autodesk ID sign-in page in a browser"
    imageCaption="The Autodesk ID sign-in page, the shared login every product would use."
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk ID is the unified authentication platform for all Autodesk
      products. The goal of any platform is common user provisioning,
      centralized data, and integrated features and workflows that strengthen
      one another. The BuildingConnected experience would benefit most from
      Docs, Sheets, and RFI integration, but Autodesk ID was a prerequisite for
      all of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Product leadership asked us to form an experience-driven “perspective” on
      the best way to integrate with Autodesk ID. They needed to understand the
      possibility space, and they were nervous about the complexity of
      integrating.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Three possible approaches">
    <p>
      The first step was to understand what was possible. I synced with a
      software architect for a knowledge transfer on how authentication might
      work, which gave us three approaches to compare. Familiar sign-in patterns
      from other products illustrated each one.
    </p>
    <p>
      With “Linked Accounts”, people would manage two accounts that share data
      and permissions, adding or removing an Autodesk ID in their user settings.
      “Twin Credentials” would let them sign in with either account, and make
      sign-up easy if they were already logged in. With “One Account”, Autodesk
      ID would replace BuildingConnected’s own authentication, and existing
      users would need to opt in, or “migrate”, to the new system.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: linkedAccountsExample,
        alt: "Facebook’s apps and websites settings, an example of linked accounts from another product",
        caption: "Linked accounts, as in Facebook’s app settings.",
      },
      {
        image: twinCredentialsExample,
        alt: "Medium’s sign-in dialog with several sign-in options, an example of twin credentials from another product",
        caption: "Twin credentials, as in Medium’s several ways to sign in.",
      },
      {
        image: oneAccountExample,
        alt: "Google’s sign-in page, an example of one account shared across many products",
        caption: "One account, as in Google’s sign-in across products.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Commit now or wait?"
    image={crossroads}
    imageAlt="A sketch of a road splitting three ways, labeled possibility 1, 2, and 3"
    imageCaption="The choice we had to stress-test: commit to one approach, or wait."
  >
    <p>
      Intuitively, One Account seemed like a strong option, but stakeholders
      wanted to be sure. Was committing to this big platform really best, or was
      there a simpler way? We needed to validate that the alternatives weren’t
      better or safer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stakeholders wanted us to “stress-test” the options, so I planned three
      rounds of prototyping and testing to identify causes of friction, mixing
      in-person hallway tests with asynchronous tests on usertesting.com. We
      focused on sign-up, for its potential to impact growth.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="What testing showed"
    layout="stacked"
    image={oneAccountPrototype}
    imageAlt="A One Account prototype: a construction drawing with a prompt to activate an Autodesk account to use a new takeoff tool"
    imageCaption="A One Account prototype, offering Autodesk ID in exchange for a new takeoff tool."
  >
    <p>
      For Linked Accounts, we tested a hypothetical messaging service where
      users were invited to view messages but had to use a secondary service to
      access attachments. It felt frustrating and redundant, and it would be
      difficult to introduce during sign-up. Twin Credentials used the same
      scenario with “twin” sign-up buttons. That removed the redundancy, but
      users saw no reason to use the secondary sign-up.
    </p>
    <p>
      For One Account, we tried getting users to migrate mid-flow by offering a
      real value proposition to real users. A clear value meant less resistance
      and friction. We also learned that there was little awareness of Autodesk,
      and that email verification was tricky.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Synthesis and insights"
    image={synthesisWorkshop}
    imageAlt="Sticky notes from a synthesis workshop, placed on a target diagram by how important they were"
    imageCaption="A synthesis workshop, sorting findings by how much they mattered."
  >
    <p>
      Over the course of exploration, prototyping, and testing, and the
      synthesis workshops I led, we arrived at some critical insights. General
      awareness of Autodesk and its products, other than AutoCAD, was low. Some
      approaches were redundant and required ongoing management. One Account
      could be inserted almost seamlessly into sign-up. And users were unlikely
      to opt in to Autodesk ID, but very open to it when it was paired with a
      new feature.
    </p>
    <p>
      With these in mind, we recommended One Account as the general approach. It
      would require a potentially disruptive prompt asking users to adopt
      Autodesk, but only as a one-time experience. BuildingConnected is a
      freemium, network-driven service, so a quick and easy sign-up is critical
      to network quality, and ultimately to growth, revenue, and user value. One
      Account avoided complicating sign-up and was easy to understand. The trick
      would be making sure existing users could migrate easily.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The invitation-to-bid flow"
    image={itbOriginal}
    imageAlt="The original invitation-to-bid page, asking the invitee to create an account to view the request for proposal"
    imageCaption="The original invitation-to-bid page, the on-ramp for new subcontractors."
  >
    <p>
      General contractors used BuildingConnected to send invitations to bid to
      subcontractors they already had relationships with. Not every
      subcontractor was on the network yet, so invitees without an account were
      prompted to sign up. That grew BuildingConnected’s network, in a virtuous
      cycle where a bigger network meant more value. It was an on-ramp not just
      to BuildingConnected but potentially to the Autodesk Construction Cloud,
      so one of my primary concerns was not to disrupt it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Invitees received an email that took them to a landing page, where they
      were prompted to sign up to see the full details of the project. If they
      entered an email that already had an account, they would just sign in.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Mapping the scenarios"
    layout="stacked"
    image={scenarioMatrix}
    imageAlt="A two-by-two matrix of BuildingConnected and Autodesk account status, showing whether each user would need to sign in or sign up, and create or link an Autodesk account"
    imageCaption="Every mix of BuildingConnected and Autodesk account, and what each person would have to do."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      With Autodesk ID, this flow became more complicated. Authentication would
      have to redirect through Autodesk, and it was unclear what to do about
      sign-ups. I created a matrix of scenarios, crossing whether someone had a
      BuildingConnected account with whether they had an Autodesk account.
    </p>
    <p>
      People who already had BuildingConnected accounts were the most likely to
      be disrupted, so we decided to handle them later. We focused first on new
      sign-ups, which were less disruptive since those users were signing up
      anyway, and asked how to make it feel like one sign-up. Long term, we
      wanted to migrate everyone to Autodesk, but redirecting new users was
      still an important step, because it stopped the growth of the population
      that would need to be migrated.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A minimal design change"
    image={itbAutodeskSignUp}
    imageAlt="The invitation-to-bid page with a branded Autodesk ID sign-up button, an “I’ve used BuildingConnected before” secondary button, and an escape-hatch sign-in link, annotated"
    imageCaption="The same page, with an Autodesk ID sign-up and a clear path for existing users."
  >
    <p>
      The updated invitation page was basically the same page, with subtle
      differences. A new, branded button to sign up with Autodesk meant people
      weren’t surprised by the redirect. I focused on not confusing legacy
      users, making it very clear how to sign in if they didn’t know what
      Autodesk ID was. The secondary button became the more intuitive “I’ve used
      BuildingConnected before”, rather than leaving people to wonder which
      account “Already have an account?” meant. Testing also showed the need for
      an “escape hatch” sign-in button for anyone the whole thing confused.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Catching existing users"
    layout="stacked"
    image={signInFlow}
    imageAlt="A flow diagram of how the invitation page routes different users to Autodesk ID sign-up and sign-in paths"
    imageCaption="How the invitation page routes each kind of user into Autodesk ID."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      Putting it all together, I mapped flow diagrams for how we would handle
      different users. We could catch whether someone already had an Autodesk
      ID, or was even already signed in.
    </p>
    <p>
      While mapping these flows, I identified a minor security vulnerability.
      Because we weren’t requiring users to verify their Autodesk account before
      accessing BuildingConnected, it would have been theoretically possible to
      “steal” someone’s BuildingConnected account. I worked with a senior
      engineer to work out how to prevent it.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="In-app prompts">
    <p>
      New users would be part of Autodesk ID automatically, but we still had to
      think about existing users, and whether we could encourage them to link to
      Autodesk right away. I designed sign-in interstitials and pop-ups to
      prompt them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The interstitial appeared to users signing in from any touch point, not
      just invitations to bid. I worked a lot on its copy: setup takes only 30
      seconds, it joins you to the larger Autodesk Construction Cloud, it adds
      enhanced security, and there is a way to learn more. Users who didn’t see
      or ignored the interstitial could also be prompted with simple pop-ups,
      which a teammate worked on in part. We exposed the prompts progressively,
      choosing cohorts of friendly companies in good standing and showing the
      prompts to increasingly larger groups.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: signInInterstitial,
        alt: "The sign-in interstitial: “Take 30 seconds to set up Autodesk ID,” with an Enable Autodesk ID button and an option to do it later",
        caption: "The sign-in interstitial: 30 seconds to set up Autodesk ID.",
      },
      {
        image: autodeskIdPopUp,
        alt: "A “Get Autodesk ID!” pop-up over the BuildingConnected bid board",
        caption: "A later pop-up, for people who skipped the interstitial.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A phased release plan"
    layout="stacked"
    image={releasePhases}
    imageAlt="The four-phase release plan, Groundwork, Pilot, Promote, and Migrate, gated by technical, UX, and adoption requirements, starting in January 2020"
    imageCaption="The release plan: Groundwork, Pilot, Promote, and Migrate, gated by thresholds rather than dates."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      Although we knew where to start, I could see that we needed a long-term
      plan. I came up with a multi-step plan for release and migration, and
      presented it to my team for feedback and buy-in. The plan had no dates.
      There was a lot of uncertainty around which integrations we wanted to
      build and the future of the Autodesk Construction Cloud, and the work was
      not directly revenue-driving, so we wanted a flexible timeline based on
      thresholds, not arbitrary dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase I, Groundwork, gave engineering time to build basic infrastructure
      and meet the technical requirements for integration. It was not a long
      phase, and I used it to design the invitation and prompt flows and explore
      future integrations. Phase II, Pilot, would manually enable Autodesk ID
      for friendly customers we knew would be OK with migrating, testing
      prototypes with them to remove UX blockers, while specific feature
      integrations were built in parallel to give users a clear value
      proposition.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase III, Promote, would actively encourage people to opt in, with
      high-value integrations as the incentive, until adoption met a threshold
      that stakeholders could choose. Phase IV, Migrate, would set a deadline
      once the bulk of users had moved and there was enough value to justify
      disrupting the rest. That meant an email and communications plan, an
      in-app countdown, removing the ways to sign in with BuildingConnected, and
      flows to catch users who missed the deadline.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The long tail"
    image={longTail}
    imageAlt="A chart of users left to migrate over time, falling steeply after the initial promotion and flattening into a long tail of dormant users"
    imageCaption="Adoption falls quickly, then flattens into a long tail of dormant accounts."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      No matter how much value we built, there would likely always be a long
      tail. After the early adopters and typical users, infrequent users might
      not sign in for a long time, and some accounts might never come back to
      BuildingConnected. We still had to handle them, just in case.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The broader unification effort"
    layout="stacked"
    image={sessionsOutOfSync}
    imageAlt="A diagram of Autodesk ID and BuildingConnected sessions falling out of sync because the two are on different domains"
    imageCaption="Separate domains let an Autodesk ID session and a BuildingConnected session fall out of sync."
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk ID was a first step in a much broader process. During my time at
      Autodesk, I also explored projects across various products, whether to
      adopt a common domain, and integrating documents and Sheets. A project was
      a central container of data and work in construction, but it worked very
      differently in different products, and the two companies’ business models
      turned out to have a huge impact on how projects and their data were
      handled. I analyzed the idea of a project across Autodesk Construction
      Cloud products, and mapped how a project’s dynamics, use cases, and
      relationships change over the lifecycle of a real-world construction
      project. That analysis showed how the conflicting business models were
      blocking unification, and how better models could improve use down-market.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also identified problems caused by separate domains, such as sign-in
      sessions falling out of sync, and presented the problem and potential
      fixes to product leadership. I eventually became something of a
      unification advocate. On a unification “road show,” I met with
      stakeholders from all over the division, sometimes learning about other
      products and other times explaining the challenges of unification and
      advocating change. I also presented my findings to product group leaders
      and to the division’s full design and research org.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>
      Today, 80% of BuildingConnected users sign in with Autodesk ID, with no
      significant drop-off in activity and no customer complaints through the
      migration. Documents and the file viewer are integrated with Autodesk.
      BuildingConnected’s product manager still shares my research artifacts
      with teams grappling with unification questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also made some tradeoffs on purpose. We moved new sign-ups first and
      left existing users for later: new users were signing up anyway, so it was
      less disruptive, and it stopped the population that needed migrating from
      growing. We relied on high-value integrations, not prompts, to give people
      a reason to switch: as expected, the in-app prompts had only a small
      impact, though they created an initial cohort to study. And the release
      plan used thresholds instead of dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      I left Autodesk in early 2022, so I didn’t see the project through. As far
      as I know, a final deadline was never enforced. Because the plan was
      flexible, the team can return to it if needed, and we avoided a forced
      migration before it was absolutely necessary.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <Banner
    variant="section"
    title="Full deck available"
    body="The original deck includes more of the research prototypes, the full sign-in flows, and the project lifecycle map."
    href="https://www.figma.com/deck/pNOmkOSvKYFaItMa8HDUWm/-case-study--Autodesk-ID?node-id=0-1&t=DjttmneWz6wHGAKZ-1"
    actionLabel="View the deck"
    image={deckCover}
    imageAlt="Title slide: Autodesk ID, Morgan Keys, Autodesk Construction Cloud, Sr. UX Designer, 2019–2021, beside a map of the sign-up flow screens"
  />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
